+# Diplomacy values and offers
+
+This document describes how the AI values trades and chooses its offers after branch
+`claude/diplomacy-values`, and the switch that turns each part on or off.
+
+## The switch
+
+`AI_DIPLOMACY_FLAGS` in `Assets/XML/BBAI_AI_Variables_GlobalDefines.xml` is a sum of the parts below.
+The default is 15, all of them. 0 gives the AI as it was before; the game then plays exactly as on
+`main` `3bec049` (checked over 150 turns in the headless harness).
+
+| Bit | Part |
+|---|---|
+| 1 | Tech values at the receiver's own research rate |
+| 2 | Resource values from their effect in the valuer's cities |
+| 4 | Offers chosen by value |
+| 8 | Tech purchases keep the gold target |
+
+## What was wrong before
+
+- **Offers were random.** Every offer the AI made started from a uniform random draw among the
+  partner's tradeable techs or spare resources: tech purchases, tech swaps, tech sales, requests for
+  help and resource swaps.
+- **Techs were priced from the raw research cost.** The receiver's research modifier was ignored:
+  tech diffusion (up to +30% from teams that know the tech) and known prerequisites.
+- **Resources were valued by fixed weights.** +100 per happiness or health, half a point per percent
+  of a building's yield bonus. Whether the AI's cities were unhappy or unhealthy, and how much
+  production and commerce its cities made, did not count. The value was scaled by the smaller of
+  the two players' city counts.
+- **Gold.** An AI with a non-negative income could trade its whole treasury for a tech.
+
+## What the AI does now
+
+**1. Tech values.** A tech's value is its remaining research divided by the receiver's research
+modifier for that tech (`CvPlayer::calculateResearchModifier`), times 1.5, plus the old premium for
+a tech few teams know.
+
+**2. Resource values.** A resource is worth what it does each turn in the valuer's own cities,
+converted as gold per turn is (2 per gold, over `PEACE_TREATY_LENGTH` turns):
+- **Happiness:** each angry citizen it lets work counts as the city's average yield per citizen;
+  half a citizen in a growing city at its happiness limit.
+- **Health:** each point of unhealthiness it removes counts as one food; half a food in a growing
+  city at its limit.
+- **Building yield bonuses:** `BonusYieldModifiers` of the buildings in the city act on the city's
+  base yields, for example Town Essentials with Coffee (+20% production, +10% commerce) or a Forge
+  with Gold (+25% commerce).
+- **Weights:** food and production count 2, commerce 1 times the city's commerce output per point of
+  commerce yield, so a Market's or Library's bonus on top counts too.
+- **Unlocks:** the units, buildings and routes that need the resource, weighted as before, scaled
+  by the valuer's own city count.
+- **Copies:** a second copy, or one of two given away, changes nothing in the cities and is worth
+  only its corporation value.
+
+When someone asks the AI for a resource, the deal must also cover what its own cities lose.
+
+**4. Offers.**
+- **Techs it asks for** (to buy, to swap, as help): the one worth most to it, four times over if
+  it is researching or has queued it, twice if it could research it now.
+- **Techs it gives in a swap:** the one whose value to the partner is closest to that.
+- **Resource swaps:** it asks for the resource it gains most from and gives the one it loses least,
+  and only if it gains more than it loses.
+- **Tech sales:** none to a team ranked above it in score.
+
+**8. Gold.** A tech purchase the AI proposes leaves its gold target (`AI_goldTarget`) untouched.
+
+## Known and not known
+
+**Known.**
+- From the headless harness (all seats AI, Mediterranean Standard, Normal speed, 150 turns): the
+  game does not crash or hang, and with the switch at 0 it plays as before.
+
+**Assumed.**
+- Food and production are worth twice a commerce point.
+- An extra citizen yields the city's average per citizen.
+- Buildings a city has not built yet do not count in the city part.
+
+**Not known.**
+- How it plays against a human. Things to look for in a game:
+  - AIs pay for techs they are researching;
+  - they keep a reserve of gold;
+  - they refuse to give a resource their cities need;
+  - they offer gold per turn for a luxury when their cities are unhappy.
+
+## Source
+
+- `CvPlayerAI.cpp`:
+  - `AI_bonusTradeVal`, `AI_bonusCityValue`, `AI_bonusUnlockVal`;
+  - `AI_techOfferValue`;
+  - the offers in `AI_doDiplo`;
+  - the loss check in `AI_considerOffer`.
+- `CvTeamAI.cpp`: `AI_techTradeVal`.
+- `CvGameCoreUtils.cpp`: `isAIDiplomacy`.
+
+Builds, the patch and the harness runs are in the GitHub repository GertaBool/RoM:
+`builds/diplomacy-values`, `fixes/diplomacy-values`.
